Given differential equation is \[\frac{d^2y}{dx^2} + 4\frac{dy}{dx} + 4y = 0\ \ \ \text{with initial conditions}\  y(0) = 1,\ y'(0)=1\] Replacing \(\dfrac{d}{dx}\) by \(D\), \[(D^2+4D+4)y = 0\] The auxiliary equation is \[m^2 + 4m + 4 = 0\] \[(m+2)^2 = 0\] Hence, the repeated root is \(m = -2\) Therefore, the general solution is \[y = (C_1 + C_2x)e^{-2x}\] Using the initial condition \(y(0)=1\), \[(C_1 + 0)e^0 = 1 \Rightarrow\ C_1 = 1\] Differentiating, \[\begin{aligned}
    y' &amp;= C_2e^{-2x} - 2(C_1 + C_2x)e^{-2x} \\ &amp;= \Bigl[C_2 - 2C_1 - 2C_2x\Bigr]e^{-2x}
\end{aligned}\] Using \(y'(0)=1\), \[(C_2 - 2C_1)e^0 = 1\] \[C_2-2 = 1 \Rightarrow\ C_2 = 3\] Hence, \[y = (1+3x)e^{-2x}\] At \(x=1\), \[y(1) = 4e^{-2} \approx 0.541\] \[\boxed{0.541}\]

