Given: \[H(f) = \begin{cases} 
e^{-j4\pi f}, &amp; |f| \leq \frac{\omega}{2} \\ 
0, &amp; |f| &gt; \frac{\omega}{2} 
\end{cases}\] So, \[|H(f)| = 1\] \[H(f) = e^{-j2 \cdot 2\pi f}\] \[H(\omega) = e^{-j2\omega} \qquad |f| \leq \frac{\omega}{2}\] \[h(t) = \delta(t - 2)\] As we know that \[y(t) = x(t) * h(t)\] \[y(t) = X(\omega) \cdot H(\omega)\] \[y(t) = x(t) * \delta(t - 2)\] \[y(t) = x(t - 2)\] \[\boxed{\text{D) x(t - 2)}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>A linear phase shift in the frequency domain corresponds to a constant time delay in the time domain.</li><li>The inverse Fourier transform of \(e^{-j\omega t_0}\) is the delayed impulse function \(\delta(t - t_0)\).</li><li>Convolution of a signal \(x(t)\) with a delayed impulse \(\delta(t - t_0)\) results in the shifted signal \(x(t - t_0)\).</li></ul></div>

The input is \[x(t)=-3e^{2t}u(t)\] so \[X(s)=\frac{-3}{s-2}.\] The system transfer function is \[G(s)=\frac{s-2}{s+3}.\] Hence, the zero-state output is \[Y_{zs}(s)=G(s)X(s)
=\frac{s-2}{s+3}\frac{-3}{s-2}
=\frac{-3}{s+3}.\] Therefore, \[y_{zs}(t)=-3e^{-3t}u(t).\] Since the initial output is \(y(0^+)=-2\), the complete response has the form \[y(t)=Ae^{-3t}-3e^{-3t}
=(A-3)e^{-3t}.\] Applying the initial condition, \[y(0^+)=A-3=-2
\quad\Rightarrow\quad A=1.\] Thus, \[y(t)=-2e^{-3t}.\] Therefore, \[y(\infty)=\lim_{t\to\infty}-2e^{-3t}=0.\] \[\boxed{0}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>The pole at \(s=2\) in the input is cancelled by the zero at \(s=2\) of \(G(s)\).</li><li>The initial condition determines the coefficient of the natural response.</li><li>Since the remaining pole is at \(s=-3\), the output decays to zero at steady state.</li></ul></div>

