To determine the probability that the batch is accepted, we analyze the sampling process: <ul><li>Total bulbs (\(N\)) = 100</li><li>Non-defective bulbs (\(G\)) = 95</li><li>Sample size (\(n\)) = 4</li></ul> The batch is accepted only if all 4 bulbs chosen are non-defective. Since the bulbs are chosen from a finite lot, this follows a hypergeometric distribution (sampling without replacement). <strong>Probability Calculation:</strong><br/>The probability is the ratio of the number of ways to choose 4 non-defective bulbs to the total number of ways to choose any 4 bulbs: \[P(\text{Accepted}) = \dfrac{^{95}C_{4}}{^{100}C_{4}}\] Expanding the combinations down to their individual structural components: \[\begin{aligned}
P(\text{Accepted}) &amp;= \dfrac{\left( \dfrac{95!}{4! \times 91!} \right)}{\left( \dfrac{100!}{4! \times 96!} \right)} \\[1.5ex]
&amp;= \dfrac{95 \times 94 \times 93 \times 92}{100 \times 99 \times 98 \times 97} \\[1.5ex]
&amp;= \dfrac{95}{100} \times \dfrac{94}{99} \times \dfrac{93}{98} \times \dfrac{92}{97}
\end{aligned}\] Solving the numerical expression: \[P(\text{Accepted}) \approx 0.8118\] \[\boxed{0.81}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Hypergeometric Distribution</strong>: This method is mandatory when the sample size is a significant portion of a finite population and sampling occurs without replacement.</li></ul></div>
